            {
              "id": "glog-c79b3c2a-b9a6-4c2d-9264-a9508adde982",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detection of Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming arises when certain functions, known to be unsafe or deprecated, are used in the code. These functions can lead to security issues such as buffer overflows, stack corruption, and other undefined behaviors. One such function is `alloca`, which allocates memory on the stack. The use of `alloca` is discouraged because it can lead to stack overflow if the allocated memory exceeds the stack size, and it does not provide a mechanism for error handling if the allocation fails.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Avoid Using `alloca`:** Replace `alloca` with safer alternatives that allocate memory on the heap, such as `malloc` or `new`, which provide better error handling and do not risk stack overflow.\n2. **Use Standard Library Containers:** Prefer using standard library containers like `std::vector` or `std::array` that manage memory automatically and provide bounds checking.\n3. **Static Analysis Tools:** Use static analysis tools to detect the use of prohibited functions and refactor the code accordingly.\n4. **Code Review:** Regularly conduct code reviews to ensure that unsafe functions are not used in the codebase.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n#### Vulnerable Code Example\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n\nvoid processData(size_t size) {\n    char* buffer = (char*)alloca(size);\n    // Use buffer\n    snprintf(buffer, size, \"Data processing\");\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", buffer);\n}\n```\n\n#### Fixed Code Example\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdlib> // Required for malloc and free\n\nvoid processData(size_t size) {\n    char* buffer = (char*)malloc(size);\n    if (buffer == nullptr) {\n        // Handle allocation failure\n        fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n        return;\n    }\n    // Use buffer\n    snprintf(buffer, size, \"Data processing\");\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", buffer);\n    free(buffer); // Free allocated memory\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstdio>`: Required for `snprintf` and `printf`.\n- `<cstdlib>`: Required for `malloc` and `free`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices - Quick Reference Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-676): Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
