            {
              "id": "glog-25c73988-df4c-4e49-ba00-047afdeddc9b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (malloc)\" vulnerability in C++ typically arises from improper use of the `malloc` function, which is used to allocate memory dynamically. In the provided code snippet, `malloc(uiNumDevices * sizeof(cl_device_id))`, the vulnerability may occur if `uiNumDevices` is not properly validated. This can lead to several issues, such as integer overflow, buffer overflow, or memory allocation failures, which can be exploited to cause a denial of service or potentially execute arbitrary code.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Ensure that `uiNumDevices` is validated to be within a reasonable range before using it in the `malloc` function.\n2. **Check for Integer Overflow**: Before performing the multiplication, check if the operation will result in an integer overflow.\n3. **Check for NULL**: Always check if the pointer returned by `malloc` is `NULL`, which indicates that the memory allocation failed.\n4. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `calloc`, which automatically initializes the allocated memory to zero, or C++ constructs like `std::vector` that manage memory more safely.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <limits>\n#include <CL/cl.h> // OpenCL library\n\nint main() {\n    size_t uiNumDevices = 10; // Example value, should be validated\n    if (uiNumDevices == 0 || uiNumDevices > std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max() / sizeof(cl_device_id)) {\n        std::cerr << \"Invalid number of devices.\" << std::endl;\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    cl_device_id* devices = (cl_device_id*)malloc(uiNumDevices * sizeof(cl_device_id));\n    if (devices == NULL) {\n        std::cerr << \"Memory allocation failed.\" << std::endl;\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    // Use the devices array...\n\n    free(devices);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- OpenCL library (`CL/cl.h`): Required for `cl_device_id` type.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html)"
