            {
              "id": "glog-b962a81b-15ea-42a1-9222-7adcc131f97c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detection of Prohibited C Function (strcpy)\" vulnerability arises from the use of the `strcpy` function in C++. This function does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. Buffer overflows can be exploited by attackers to execute arbitrary code, cause a program crash, or corrupt data.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or C++ string handling functions.\n2. **Bounds Checking**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string, including the null terminator.\n3. **Use C++ Strings**: Prefer using `std::string` over C-style strings to leverage automatic memory management and bounds checking.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nGiven the specific vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\nstrcpy(temp_filename_cstr, temp_filename->c_str());\n```\n\nA safer alternative using `strncpy` would be:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(temp_filename_cstr, temp_filename->c_str(), sizeof(temp_filename_cstr) - 1);\ntemp_filename_cstr[sizeof(temp_filename_cstr) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\nOr, using C++ string handling:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string temp_filename_str = *temp_filename;\nstrncpy(temp_filename_cstr, temp_filename_str.c_str(), sizeof(temp_filename_cstr) - 1);\ntemp_filename_cstr[sizeof(temp_filename_cstr) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: For `strncpy` function.\n- `<string>`: For `std::string` class.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
