            {
              "id": "glog-7f73ee90-2826-4427-baae-bcd79473cc05",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Unsecured URL\" vulnerability in C++ programming occurs when a program uses URLs without proper validation or security measures, potentially exposing the application to various attacks such as man-in-the-middle attacks, data interception, or redirection to malicious sites. This vulnerability is particularly critical when dealing with sensitive data or performing critical operations over the network.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Use HTTPS**: Always use HTTPS instead of HTTP to ensure data is encrypted during transmission.\n2. **Validate URLs**: Implement strict validation checks on URLs to ensure they conform to expected patterns and domains.\n3. **Use Secure Libraries**: Utilize libraries that provide secure network communication features.\n4. **Input Sanitization**: Sanitize all inputs that are used to construct URLs to prevent injection attacks.\n5. **Error Handling**: Implement robust error handling to manage unexpected URL formats or network issues.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is a C++ code example demonstrating how to securely handle URLs using the `libcurl` library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <curl/curl.h>\n\nsize_t WriteCallback(void* contents, size_t size, size_t nmemb, void* userp) {\n    ((std::string*)userp)->append((char*)contents, size * nmemb);\n    return size * nmemb;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    CURL* curl;\n    CURLcode res;\n    std::string readBuffer;\n\n    curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_DEFAULT);\n    curl = curl_easy_init();\n    if(curl) {\n        // Use HTTPS instead of HTTP\n        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, \"https://yann.lecun.com\");\n        \n        // Set up a callback function to handle the response\n        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, WriteCallback);\n        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_WRITEDATA, &readBuffer);\n\n        // Perform the request\n        res = curl_easy_perform(curl);\n\n        // Check for errors\n        if(res != CURLE_OK) {\n            fprintf(stderr, \"curl_easy_perform() failed: %s\\n\", curl_easy_strerror(res));\n        } else {\n            std::cout << readBuffer << std::endl;\n        }\n\n        // Clean up\n        curl_easy_cleanup(curl);\n    }\n    curl_global_cleanup();\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- `libcurl`: A free and easy-to-use client-side URL transfer library, supporting a range of protocols including HTTPS.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices - Quick Reference Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
